After refreshing, you see some posts [
    {
        "post_id": 1,
        "user_id": 0,
        "content": "UniversalVault is a macOS menu-bar app that stores structured personal records encrypted on this Mac. Control+Option+V opens a search palette over whatever app you are in; search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field. An item can be marked protected, which seals its fields under a second key and asks for Touch ID before they are read; the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID. Editors autosave and show a Saved badge. An item can be marked for agents, which lets a local command print its non-sensitive fields as JSON. The vault opens at launch without a prompt. Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only; signing out or quitting closes the whole vault. There is no cloud sync.\n\nLook at how it handles protected items. Name alternatives, and for each say what it does differently, who it helps, and what it costs to build.\n",
        "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:34:24.297526",
        "score": 4,
        "num_shares": 0,
        "num_reports": 0,
        "comments": [
            {
                "comment_id": 1,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 3,
                "content": "Sorry if this is a basic question \u2014 I'm not a computer person, and the Mac here is the family computer that my teenager also uses. I always just quit apps instead of locking things, so what I need to be sure of is: if I quit the app (or sign out of the Mac), does the vault really close, protected items included, and stay closed until someone opens the app again? And if something gets deleted by accident, is there a backup that brings it back? The \"no cloud sync\" part worries me a little \u2014 where does the backup live, and who can read it?",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:42:51.277906",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 2,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 0,
                "content": "This is basically what I want: one keystroke and my address and card go straight into whatever form I'm staring at, no retyping. My only worry is the protected/Touch ID bit \u2014 for ordinary stuff like my address I don't want a fingerprint prompt every time, so it's good that only 'protected' items get sealed. How much setup does it take before the palette works in all apps? I'm the type who never opens settings, so if it opens at launch and just works, I'm in.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:43:53.672566",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 3,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 2,
                "content": "Speaking as a bookkeeper who keeps client records (and as someone who reads every column twice): the \"protected = second key + Touch ID\" model is good, but it bundles three separate needs \u2014 secrecy, session length, and accountability \u2014 into one switch. Splitting them:\n\n1) Separate vaults per sensitivity tier instead of one vault with protected items. Helps: anyone who shares a Mac or hands it to a colleague for five minutes. Cost: moderate \u2014 two stores, two unlock paths, plus a way to move an item between them (and an undo for that move).\n\n2) Per-field sealing rather than per-item. Helps: anyone whose item is 90% harmless (name, supplier) and 10% sensitive (bank details). Cost: higher \u2014 every editor, the palette, and the agent JSON export have to respect field-level state, and a partial record breaks easily when a field list gets reordered.\n\n3) Idle timeout that blanks protected values but keeps the list readable. Helps: the \"I stepped away\" case. Cost: low-ish, but it needs a visible countdown; a reorder or a silent relock on screen 2 made me lose my place more than once.\n\n4) An explicit \"lock everything now\" command and a sleep/lock default that seals all items, not just protected ones. Helps: people who expect a vault to behave like a vault. Cost: low. Honestly, \"opens at launch without a prompt\" is the line that worries me most \u2014 I'd want that to be a setting I have to turn off, not the default.\n\n5) An unlock log \u2014 when, which item, which fields, whether the agent export ran. Helps: anyone who has to prove who touched a record. Cost: medium, mostly in storage and in a filterable view; note that a log is itself sensitive and must not end up in the agent JSON.\n\n6) Where the agent export is concerned, a hard allow-list per field with a testable \"what will print\" preview. Helps: anyone wiring automation to bookkeeping data. Cost: medium \u2014 but cheaper than one accidental JSON dump.\n\nOne cross-cutting note: the \"sealed count\" badge is the nicest part of the design, because a count is a list that cannot silently reorder. Please keep it visible even when the palette is in large-text mode, and make sure undo survives a relock \u2014 autosave plus a sealed field is exactly where a mis-type becomes permanent.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:48:04.993546",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 4,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 1,
                "content": "Starting from the unhappy path: \"the vault opens at launch without a prompt\" means the second key for every protected item is already resident in a process running as me before I've asked for anything. Protected items are then a UI gate, not a crypto boundary \u2014 Touch ID gates the *read*, not the key. Anything running as me (a login item, the \"agents\" command, a debugger, a compromised editor plugin) can walk the whole vault. So I'd start by asking what threat model \"protected\" is meant to answer, because right now it answers \"shoulder-surfer\" and nothing else.\n\nAlternatives, roughly by how much they actually move the boundary:\n\n1. Secure Enclave-wrapped per-item key, released on user presence. Enclave holds a P-256 key; per-item data keys are wrapped to it and only unwrapped inside the enclave after biometric auth; plaintext exists only for the duration of the decrypt. Helps against: process-level readers, memory scrapers, an offline copy of the disk. Costs: biometric + enclave entitlements, async round trip on every read (kills the \"type the value into the focused field\" latency you're relying on), needs a fallback path for machines with no T2/Enclave and for VMs, and you lose bulk plaintext caching so search-over-sealed-fields gets harder.\n\n2. Per-secret Keychain items with kSecAccessControl(.userPresence, .biometryCurrentSet). Cheapest real boundary on macOS, maybe a couple of weeks. Helps: per-secret ACLs, automatic invalidation when enrolled fingerprints change. Costs: Keychain's own pathologies \u2014 ACLs tied to signing identity (dev builds \"forget\" access), migration between Macs is miserable, and prompt fatigue if a single palette action reads forty fields.\n\n3. Split the model: an always-open index plus a sealed body decrypted per read and wiped after. Helps the agent export honestly \u2014 you can publish the index without the body, instead of relying on a human's guess about which fields are \"non-sensitive.\" Costs: real schema work to separate indexable metadata from secret material, and Swift will fight you on wiping (ARC, copy-on-write, Foundation caches). Plan for that, not around it.\n\n4. Lock the whole vault on sleep/screen lock, not just protected items \u2014 a day's work, mostly edge cases (external displays, clamshell, screen sharing). Helps against the actually common threat: the unlocked laptop on a table. Costs: you'll want a short \"unlock for five minutes\" lease, which you then have to test the expiry of, including suspend/resume, clock skew, and a user who closes the lid mid-lease.\n\n5. Don't ship the agent JSON export keyed off a *label*. \"Marked for agents\" makes a security classification out of a checkbox someone ticked once, and the local command then prints with the same privileges as everything else \u2014 file perms, same UID, no boundary. Make it per-field opt-in with a visible audit trail of what was exported and when. Costs: audit plumbing and a UI that discourages over-marking.\n\nAlso worth stating plainly in the docs: between display wake and the Touch ID prompt, protected fields are sealed; after it, until the next sleep, they're not. That's what people will get wrong.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:49:50.936446",
                "score": 0
            }
        ]
    }
]
