                "content": "Starting from the unhappy path: \"the vault opens at launch without a prompt\" means the second key for every protected item is already resident in a process running as me before I've asked for anything. Protected items are then a UI gate, not a crypto boundary \u2014 Touch ID gates the *read*, not the key. Anything running as me (a login item, the \"agents\" command, a debugger, a compromised editor plugin) can walk the whole vault. So I'd start by asking what threat model \"protected\" is meant to answer, because right now it answers \"shoulder-surfer\" and nothing else.\n\nAlternatives, roughly by how much they actually move the boundary:\n\n1. Secure Enclave-wrapped per-item key, released on user presence. Enclave holds a P-256 key; per-item data keys are wrapped to it and only unwrapped inside the enclave after biometric auth; plaintext exists only for the duration of the decrypt. Helps against: process-level readers, memory scrapers, an offline copy of the disk. Costs: biometric + enclave entitlements, async round trip on every read (kills the \"type the value into the focused field\" latency you're relying on), needs a fallback path for machines with no T2/Enclave and for VMs, and you lose bulk plaintext caching so search-over-sealed-fields gets harder.\n\n2. Per-secret Keychain items with kSecAccessControl(.userPresence, .biometryCurrentSet). Cheapest real boundary on macOS, maybe a couple of weeks. Helps: per-secret ACLs, automatic invalidation when enrolled fingerprints change. Costs: Keychain's own pathologies \u2014 ACLs tied to signing identity (dev builds \"forget\" access), migration between Macs is miserable, and prompt fatigue if a single palette action reads forty fields.\n\n3. Split the model: an always-open index plus a sealed body decrypted per read and wiped after. Helps the agent export honestly \u2014 you can publish the index without the body, instead of relying on a human's guess about which fields are \"non-sensitive.\" Costs: real schema work to separate indexable metadata from secret material, and Swift will fight you on wiping (ARC, copy-on-write, Foundation caches). Plan for that, not around it.\n\n4. Lock the whole vault on sleep/screen lock, not just protected items \u2014 a day's work, mostly edge cases (external displays, clamshell, screen sharing). Helps against the actually common threat: the unlocked laptop on a table. Costs: you'll want a short \"unlock for five minutes\" lease, which you then have to test the expiry of, including suspend/resume, clock skew, and a user who closes the lid mid-lease.\n\n5. Don't ship the agent JSON export keyed off a *label*. \"Marked for agents\" makes a security classification out of a checkbox someone ticked once, and the local command then prints with the same privileges as everything else \u2014 file perms, same UID, no boundary. Make it per-field opt-in with a visible audit trail of what was exported and when. Costs: audit plumbing and a UI that discourages over-marking.\n\nAlso worth stating plainly in the docs: between display wake and the Touch ID prompt, protected fields are sealed; after it, until the next sleep, they're not. That's what people will get wrong.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:49:50.936446",
                "score": 0
            }
        ]
    }
]
